const estateNarrative = {
  eyebrow: 'THE DISPERSION PROBLEM',
  headline: 'Estate Appliances Fail Quietly, in Buildings Nobody Checks',
  paragraphs: [
    "Count compressors on a Covenant parcel and you'll usually pass six before you leave the main house: the kitchen column, the pantry drawers, the bar's beverage center, then the guest house, the cabana, the garage freezer, the wine room. Each unit has its own duty cycle and its own environment — and the further a unit sits from daily life, the longer its problems go unnoticed. The classic RSF discovery isn't a breakdown; it's a guest opening a warm refrigerator that's been failing since spring.",
    "Dispersion also changes the economics of a service visit. Once a technician has cleared the gate and reached the property, looking at a second and third unit costs a fraction of a separate call — so we encourage managers to keep a running list and empty it every visit. The urgent repair pays for the trip; the batched inspections are where an estate's appliance budget actually gets efficient. The properties that hand us a standing spring-and-fall route through their outbuildings trade surprise breakdowns for line items — which is the whole point of having a budget.",
    "And when a unit is genuinely done — a decades-old outbuilding refrigerator with a failed sealed system, say — we say so before the repair, not after it. Estate managers keep working with companies that protect their credibility with the owner. That's the entire long game."
  ]
};

